          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category+equipped+with+proarrows"} 2-category equipped with proarrows
            is a 2-category together with a 2-category of “proarrows” which are intended to generalize the arrows of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            in the same way that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/profunctors"} profunctors
            generalize the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functors"} functors
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorification"} categorification
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relations"} relations
            = succeed "." do
              %em 1-category equipped with relations
          %h2#definition Definition
          %h3#12categories_equipped_with_proarrows (1,2)-categories equipped with proarrows
          %p
            Recall that a
            %em 2-category equipped with proarrows
            (aka “proarrow equipment” or “equipment”) can be defined as a certain sort of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+category"} double category
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒱
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %munder
                      %mi K
                      %mo ̲
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{V}(\underline{K}) = K
            = succeed "," do
              %strong (1,2)-category equipped with proarrows
            = succeed "." do
              %strong (1,2)-category proarrow equipment
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+posetal+2-category"} locally posetal
          %p
            For example, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            is any
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantale"} quantale
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V Cat
            is naturally a (1,2)-category equipped with proarrows. In particular, taking
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                    %mo =
                    %mi 𝟚
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V=\mathbb{2}
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/preorders"} preorders
          %h3#1categories_equipped_with_relations 1-categories equipped with relations
          %p
            A
            %strong 1-category equipped with relations
            is a (1,2)-category equipped with proarrows, regarded as a double category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %munder
                      %mi K
                      %mo ̲
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \underline{K}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/involution"} involution
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %munder
                      %mi K
                      %mo ̲
                    %mrow
                      %mi h
                      %mi op
                  %mo ≅
                  %munder
                    %mi K
                    %mo ̲
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \underline{K}^{h op} \cong \underline{K}
            which is (isomorphic to) the identity on objects and (vertical) arrows. Here
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %munder
                      %mi K
                      %mo ̲
                    %mrow
                      %mi h
                      %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \underline{K}^{h op}
            denotes the horizontal opposite of a double category obtained by reversing the horizontal (pro-)arrows but not the vertical ones. We also call this structure a
            %strong relation equipment
            or a
            = succeed "." do
              %strong 1-category proarrow equipment
          %p
            In particular, the definition implies that we have an involution
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                  %mo ≅
                  %msup
                    %mi K
                    %mi co
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K \cong K^{co}
            which is the identity on objects and arrows, which for a (1,2)-category means that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            is actually (equivalent to) a 1-category. Note though that the 2-category of proarrows (which we now call “relations”) is still (like
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Rel"} Rel
          %p
            For example, for any quantale
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V Cat
            consisting of the
            %em symmetric
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi y
                    %mo ,
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A(x,y) = A(y,x)
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                    %mo =
                    %mi 𝟚
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V=\mathbb{2}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %munder
                    %mi Rel
                    %mo ̲
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \underline{Rel}
            of sets, functions, and binary relations.
          %p
            In general, we can think of a relation equipment as generalizing some of the properties of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %munder
                      %mi Rel
                      %mo ̲
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \underline{Rel}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+category"} regular category
            also form a relation equipment.
          %h2#cartesian_1categories_equipped_with_relations Cartesian 1-categories equipped with relations
          %p It is proven in
          %ul
            %li
              Carboni, Kelly, Wood, “A 2-categorical approach to change of base and geometric morphisms, I” (
              %a{:href => "http://archive.numdam.org/ARCHIVE/CTGDC/CTGDC_1991__32_1/CTGDC_1991__32_1_47_0/CTGDC_1991__32_1_47_0.pdf"}> PDF
              )
          %p
            that a (1,2)-category is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+bicategory"} cartesian bicategory
            precisely when it is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+object"} cartesian object
            in a suitable 2-category of proarrow equipments (where we make a bicategory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            into an equipment by taking the proarrows to be those of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            and the arrows to be the “maps” in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
          .un_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %munder
                      %mi K
                      %mo ̲
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \underline{K}
              be a 1-category equipped with relations, which is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+object"} cartesian object
              in the 2-category of relation equipments (that is, it is a
              = succeed ")." do
                %strong cartesian relation equipment
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℋ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %munder
                      %mi K
                      %mo ̲
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{H}(\underline{K})
              is a cartesian bicategory.
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              That
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %munder
                      %mi K
                      %mo ̲
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \underline{K}
              is a cartesian object means, in particular, that it is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudomonoid"} pseudomonoid
              in the 2-category of equipments. By lifting the coherence data from arrows to representable proarrows, it follows that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℋ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %munder
                      %mi K
                      %mo ̲
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{H}(\underline{K})
              is a monoidal 2-category. Being a cartesian object also gives a cartesian product on objects and proarrows, with diagonals
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Δ
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mi X
                      %mo ×
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta\colon X\to X\times X
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Δ
                      %mo •
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta_\bullet
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Δ
                      %mo •
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta^\bullet
              gives each object a commutative monoid and comonoid structure. Now for any proarrow
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi\colon X\to Y
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mi ϕ
                          %mtd
                            %mi Y
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo
                              %mi Δ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                            %mo ⇓
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mi Δ
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                            %mo ×
                            %mi X
                          %mtd
                            %munder
                              %mo →
                              %mrow
                                %mi ϕ
                                %mo ×
                                %mi ϕ
                          %mtd
                            %mi Y
                            %mo ×
                            %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{X &amp; \overset{\phi}{\to} &amp; Y\\
                    ^\Delta \downarrow &amp; \Downarrow &amp; \downarrow^\Delta\\
                    X\times X&amp; \underset{\phi\times \phi}{\to} &amp; Y\times Y}
            %p
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %munder
                      %mi K
                      %mo ̲
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \underline{K}
              induces 2-cells, i.e. inequalities,
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Δ
                      %mo •
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo ≤
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo ×
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msub
                      %mi Δ
                      %mo •
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta_\bullet \phi \le (\phi\times\phi)\Delta_\bullet
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ϕ
                      %msup
                        %mi Δ
                        %mo •
                      %mo ≤
                      %msup
                        %mi Δ
                        %mo •
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo ×
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi \Delta^\bullet  \le \Delta^\bullet(\phi\times\phi)
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bicategory+of+relations"} bicategory of relations
            is a (1,2)-category which is a cartesian bicategory, and which also satisfies some additional conditions. We can also construct this structure starting from a relation equipment.
          .un_theorem
            %h6#theorem_2 Theorem
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %munder
                      %mi K
                      %mo ̲
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \underline{K}
              be a relation equipment satisfying the hypotheses of the previous theorem, and suppose in addition that every proarrow
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi x
                    %mo ↛
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi\colon x\nrightarrow y
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %munder
                      %mi K
                      %mo ̲
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \underline{K}
              can be written as
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mo •
                    %msup
                      %mi g
                      %mo •
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_\bullet g^\bullet
              for some (vertical) arrows
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℋ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %munder
                      %mi K
                      %mo ̲
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{H}(\underline{K})
              is a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bicategory+of+relations"} bicategory of relations
          .proof
            %h6#sketch_of_proof Sketch of Proof
            %p
              We first verify the axiom
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi Δ
                        %mo •
                      %msub
                        %mi Δ
                        %mo •
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta^\bullet \Delta_\bullet = 1
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Δ
                      %mo •
                    %msub
                      %mi Δ
                      %mo •
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta^\bullet \Delta_\bullet
              is the restriction of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mn 1
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                        %mo ×
                        %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1_{X\times X}
              along
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Δ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta
              on both sides, it suffices to show that
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mn 1
                                  %mi X
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo
                              %mi Δ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                            %mo ⇓
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mi Δ
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                            %mo ×
                            %mi X
                          %mtd
                            %munder
                              %mo →
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mn 1
                                  %mrow
                                    %mi X
                                    %mo ×
                                    %mi X
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                            %mo ×
                            %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{X &amp; \overset{1_X}{\to} &amp; X\\
                    ^\Delta\downarrow &amp;\Downarrow&amp; \downarrow^\Delta\\
                    X\times X&amp; \underset{1_{X\times X}}{\to} &amp; X\times X}
            %p
              is a cartesian 2-cell in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %munder
                        %mi K
                        %mo ̲
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \underline{K}
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi A
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mi ϕ
                          %mtd
                            %mi B
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo
                              %mrow
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mi f
                                %mo ,
                                %mi g
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                            %mo ⇓
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mrow
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mi h
                                %mo ,
                                %mi k
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                            %mo ×
                            %mi X
                          %mtd
                            %munder
                              %mo →
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mn 1
                                  %mrow
                                    %mi X
                                    %mo ×
                                    %mi X
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                            %mo ×
                            %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{A &amp; \overset{\phi}{\to} &amp; B\\
                    ^{(f,g)}\downarrow &amp;\Downarrow&amp; \downarrow^{(h,k)}\\
                    X\times X&amp; \underset{1_{X\times X}}{\to} &amp; X\times X}
            %p
              then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo ,
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (f,g)
              factoring through
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Δ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta
              means that
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo =
                      %mi g
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f=g
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi h
                      %mo =
                      %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h=k
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                            %mo ×
                            %mi X
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mn 1
                                  %mrow
                                    %mi X
                                    %mo ×
                                    %mi X
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                            %mo ×
                            %mi X
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                            %mo ⇓
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                          %mtd
                            %munder
                              %mo →
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mn 1
                                  %mi X
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{X\times X &amp; \overset{1_{X\times X}}{\to} &amp; X\times X\\
                    \\downarrow &amp;\Downarrow &amp; \downarrow\\
                    X&amp; \underset{1_X}{\to} &amp; X}
            %p
              (which comes from being a cartesian object in
              = succeed ")," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Equipments
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Equipments
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi A
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mi ϕ
                          %mtd
                            %mi B
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo
                              %mi f
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                            %mo ⇓
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mi g
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                          %mtd
                            %munder
                              %mo →
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mn 1
                                  %mi X
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{A &amp; \overset{\phi}{\to} &amp; B \\
                    ^f\downarrow &amp;\Downarrow &amp; \downarrow^g\\
                    X&amp; \underset{1_X}{\to} &amp; X}
            %p which factors the given square through the putative cartesian one. The factorization is unique since all 2-cells are unique.
            %p
              We now verify the Frobenius axiom
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi Δ
                        %mo •
                      %msub
                        %mi Δ
                        %mo •
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ×
                      %msub
                        %mi Δ
                        %mo •
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msup
                        %mi Δ
                        %mo •
                      %mo ×
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta^\bullet \Delta_\bullet = (1\times \Delta_\bullet)(\Delta^\bullet \times 1)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Δ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta
              is associative, we have a square
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mn 1
                                  %mi X
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo
                              %mi Δ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mi Δ
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                            %mo ×
                            %mi X
                          %mtd
                            %mo ⇓
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                            %mo ×
                            %mi X
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo
                              %mrow
                                %mn 1
                                %mo ×
                                %mi Δ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mrow
                                %mi Δ
                                %mo ×
                                %mn 1
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                            %mo ×
                            %mi X
                            %mo ×
                            %mi X
                          %mtd
                            %munder
                              %mo →
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mn 1
                                  %mrow
                                    %mi X
                                    %mo ×
                                    %mi X
                                    %mo ×
                                    %mi X
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                            %mo ×
                            %mi X
                            %mo ×
                            %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{X &amp; \overset{1_X}{\to} &amp; X\\
                    ^\Delta\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow^\Delta\\
                    X\times X &amp; \Downarrow &amp; X\times X\\
                    ^{1\times \Delta}\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow^{\Delta\times 1}\\
                    X\times X\times X &amp; \underset{1_{X\times X\times X}}{\to} &amp; X\times X\times X}
            %p and therefore a square
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                            %mo ×
                            %mi X
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mrow
                                %msup
                                  %mi Δ
                                  %mo •
                                %msub
                                  %mi Δ
                                  %mo •
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                            %mo ×
                            %mi X
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo
                              %mrow
                                %mn 1
                                %mo ×
                                %mi Δ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mrow
                                %mi Δ
                                %mo ×
                                %mn 1
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                            %mo ×
                            %mi X
                            %mo ×
                            %mi X
                          %mtd
                            %munder
                              %mo →
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mn 1
                                  %mrow
                                    %mi X
                                    %mo ×
                                    %mi X
                                    %mo ×
                                    %mi X
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                            %mo ×
                            %mi X
                            %mo ×
                            %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{X\times X &amp; \overset{\Delta^\bullet \Delta_\bullet}{\to} &amp; X\times  X\\
                    ^{1\times \Delta}\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow^{\Delta\times 1}\\
                    X\times X\times X &amp; \underset{1_{X\times X\times X}}{\to} &amp; X\times X\times X}
            %p and it suffices to show that this is a cartesian 2-cell. So suppose given a square
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi A
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mi ϕ
                          %mtd
                            %mi B
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo
                              %mrow
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mi f
                                %mo ,
                                %mi g
                                %mo ,
                                %mi g
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                            %mo ⇓
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mrow
